
"smart_sources:- Inventing the Street.md": {"path":"- Inventing the Street.md","last_embed":{"hash":null},"embeddings":{},"last_read":{"hash":"139jt3","at":1785794982964},"class_name":"SmartSource","last_import":{"mtime":1783981326723,"size":4332,"at":1785794983303,"hash":"139jt3"},"blocks":{"#---frontmatter---":[1,8],"#":[9,45]},"outlinks":[{"title":"horse in an ox harness","target":"https://engines.egr.uh.edu/sites/engines/files/images/page/oxyokeonhorse.jpg","line":25,"embedded":true},{"title":"852 Holly Avenue","target":"https://engines.egr.uh.edu/sites/engines/files/images/page/852holly.gif","line":43,"embedded":true}],"metadata":{"title":"Inventing the Street","source":"https://engines.egr.uh.edu/episode/1913","author":null,"published":null,"created":"2026-07-13","description":"Today, we invent the city street. The University of Houston's College of Engineering presents this series about the machines that make our civilization run, and the people whose ingenuity created them. As a child, growing into an awareness of the world around me, I was first conscious of my room and my parents, then of my house and the street in front. Next, I learned that I lived on a grid. My personal coordinate was 852 Holly Avenue. To the west, Holly intersected Victoria Street, to the east Avon."},"task_lines":[],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[]},"smart_blocks:- Inventing the Street.md#---frontmatter---": {"path":null,"last_embed":{"hash":null},"embeddings":{},"text":null,"length":0,"last_read":{"hash":"14xf3sf","at":1785794982964},"key":"- Inventing the Street.md#---frontmatter---","lines":[1,8],"size":647,"outlinks":[],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},
